{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And you shall show them no favor.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this word in many ways: from the root חן (favor), from the root חניה (dwelling), and from the root חנם (gratis) — apart from its plain sense, which is the language of חנינה (grace), as if it said 'you shall not show them grace.' And so they expounded in tractate Avodah Zarah: 'לא תחנם — you shall not give them favor': it is forbidden for a person to say, 'How fine this gentile is,' as it is said לא תחנם — read it לא תִחְנֵם, with the חי\"ת in חיר\"ק. And they further expounded: לא תַחְנֵם — you shall not give them dwelling on land, for it is forbidden to sell them land in the land of Israel, because this would be a cause for them to settle there, and the Torah said (Exodus 23:33): ״לא ישבו בארצך פן יחטיאו אותך לי״ — read it לא תַחְנֵם, with the תי\"ו in פת\"ח and the חי\"ת in שו\"א and פת\"ח. And they further expounded: לא תַחְנֵּם — you shall not give them a free gift — read it לא תַחְנֵּם, with the חי\"ת in פת\"ח and the נו\"ן with a dagesh. And we learn from here how great is the power of the Torah, that it is expounded for many meanings, to the point that even a single word is interpreted for several senses according to the vowel points; and as the vowel points move, the word moves. For the letters are the body and the vowel point is the soul; and so they said: the vowel points in the letters of the Torah of Moses are like the soul of life in the body of man. And it is known that a body has no movement without the soul, and when the soul moves, the body moves in every direction and every side — so too the vowel points in the letters of the Torah: with the change of the vowel points, the meaning changes. And therefore it was necessary for the matter in a Torah scroll to be without vowel points, so that the Torah may be interpreted in many ways beyond the single intent of the verse. And from here the power of the great Name in its letters will be explained to you, for its meaning changes according to the change of its vowel points. And I have already written of this in the verse (Numbers 11:15) ״ואם ככה את עושה לי״, and this is clear to every understanding person."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:21.773022281Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}